---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import ElectrochemSim from '../../simulators/electrochem/ElectrochemSim.astro';
import { electrochemQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/electrochem';

const toc = [
  { id: 'cells', label: 'Two kinds of electrochemical cell' },
  { id: 'galvanic', label: 'Inside a galvanic cell' },
  { id: 'potentials', label: 'Electrode potentials and cell voltage' },
  { id: 'running-down', label: 'Why batteries run down' },
  { id: 'electrolysis', label: 'Electrolysis' },
  { id: 'faraday', label: 'How much product? Faraday’s law' },
  { id: 'compare', label: 'Galvanic vs electrolytic' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Chapter 17: Electrochemistry (17.2 Galvanic Cells; 17.3 Electrode and Cell Potentials; 17.4 Potential, Free Energy, and Equilibrium; 17.7 Electrolysis). OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-introduction' },
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Appendix L: Standard Electrode (Half-Cell) Potentials. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/l-standard-electrode-half-cell-potentials' },
  { text: 'Bard, A. J., Parsons, R., & Jordan, J. (1985). Standard Potentials in Aqueous Solution. IUPAC / Marcel Dekker.' },
  { text: 'Bard, A. J., & Faulkner, L. R. (2001). Electrochemical Methods: Fundamentals and Applications (2nd ed.). Wiley.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="chemistry"
  topic="electrochemistry"
  description="Interactive electrochemistry simulator: build galvanic cells from six metals, watch electrons and ions move and the voltage fall, then run electrolysis of water, copper chloride and copper refining."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={electrochemQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A battery turns a chemical reaction into electricity; electrolysis does the opposite and uses electricity to force a
      reaction. Both are redox reactions with the oxidation and reduction pulled apart into two electrodes. Build a cell
      from two metals, light a bulb with it, then switch on a power supply and split water into hydrogen and oxygen.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <ElectrochemSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>In <strong>Galvanic</strong> mode, choose a metal for each electrode. The simulator works out which one is the <strong>anode</strong> and which the <strong>cathode</strong>, and the voltmeter shows the cell voltage.</li>
      <li>Switch <strong>Connect</strong> to <strong>Light bulb</strong> and press <strong>Start</strong>. Watch electrons (yellow) flow through the wire, ions move in the salt bridge, the electrodes change mass and the voltage graph slowly fall.</li>
      <li>Change the <strong>concentrations</strong> to see how they shift the voltage.</li>
      <li>Switch to <strong>Electrolytic</strong>, choose a solution and change the <strong>power supply voltage</strong>. Below the onset voltage nothing happens; above it, products build up in proportion to the charge passed.</li>
      <li>Click the electrodes, salt bridge, meter or power supply for explanations. <strong>Fresh electrodes and solutions</strong> starts the current cell again; <strong>Reset</strong> restores everything.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="cells">Two kinds of electrochemical cell</h2>
  <p>
    In a redox reaction, electrons pass from a reducing agent to an oxidizing agent. If the two are put in separate
    containers joined by a wire, the electrons must travel through the wire: an electric current. A
    <strong>galvanic</strong> (or voltaic) cell uses a reaction that happens by itself to produce electricity. An
    <strong>electrolytic</strong> cell uses electricity from a power supply to drive a reaction that would not happen by
    itself. In both, <strong>oxidation happens at the anode and reduction at the cathode</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="galvanic">Inside a galvanic cell</h2>
  <p>
    The classic example is the <strong>Daniell cell</strong>: a zinc electrode in zinc sulfate solution and a copper
    electrode in copper(II) sulfate solution.
  </p>
  <code class="formula">Anode (oxidation): Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻</code>
  <code class="formula">Cathode (reduction): Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</code>
  <p>
    Zinc atoms leave the anode as ions, and the electrons they leave behind make it the <strong>negative</strong> electrode.
    The electrons flow through the external circuit to the copper cathode, the <strong>positive</strong> electrode, where
    copper ions pick them up and are deposited as copper. Inside the cell, the current is carried by ions. The
    <strong>salt bridge</strong>, a tube of salt solution or gel, lets negative ions drift towards the anode and positive
    ions towards the cathode, keeping both solutions neutral. Electrons never travel through the solutions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="potentials">Electrode potentials and cell voltage</h2>
  <p>
    Each half-cell has a <strong>standard reduction potential</strong>, E°, measured against a standard hydrogen
    electrode at 25 °C with 1 mol/L solutions. The more negative E° is, the more easily the metal gives up electrons.
    The half-cell with the lower potential becomes the anode, and the cell voltage is:
  </p>
  <code class="formula">E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode</code>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Half-reaction</th><th>E° (V)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Mg</td><td>−2.37</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Zn</td><td>−0.76</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Fe</td><td>−0.44</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb</td><td>−0.13</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</td><td>+0.34</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag</td><td>+0.80</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    For zinc and copper, E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.76) = 1.10 V. Metals far apart in the list make bigger voltages. Note that
    E° does not depend on how many electrons are involved: the silver half-reaction is not doubled when it is balanced.
  </p>

  <h2 id="running-down">Why batteries run down</h2>
  <p>
    The voltage also depends on concentration, through the <strong>Nernst equation</strong>. At 25 °C, for a metal ion of
    charge n:
  </p>
  <code class="formula">E = E° + (0.0257 / n) × ln[Mⁿ⁺]</code>
  <p>
    As the cell works, cathode ions are used up and anode ions build up, so the two electrode potentials move closer and
    the voltage falls. When the reaction reaches equilibrium, the voltage is zero: the battery is flat. The simulator
    shows this on the voltage graph.
  </p>

  <h2 id="electrolysis">Electrolysis</h2>
  <p>
    In electrolysis, a power supply pushes electrons into the <strong>cathode</strong>, which is now
    <strong>negative</strong>, and pulls them from the <strong>anode</strong>, which is <strong>positive</strong>. Positive
    ions move to the cathode and are reduced; negative ions or water molecules are oxidized at the anode. The supply must
    provide at least the cell’s reversed voltage, plus extra “overpotential” to make the reactions go at a useful
    rate. Water needs at least 1.23 V to split, but in practice about 1.7 V or more.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faraday">How much product? Faraday’s law</h2>
  <p>
    Every electron passed reduces or oxidizes a fixed amount of substance. One mole of electrons carries 96,485 coulombs,
    the <strong>Faraday constant</strong> F.
  </p>
  <code class="formula">charge Q = current I × time t</code>
  <code class="formula">moles of product = Q ÷ (z × F)</code>
  <p>
    Here z is the number of electrons per particle of product (2 for Cu²⁺ → Cu or for H₂, 4 for O₂). That is why
    electrolysis of water gives twice the volume of hydrogen as oxygen.
  </p>

  <h2 id="compare">Galvanic vs electrolytic</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Galvanic cell</th><th>Electrolytic cell</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Energy</td><td>Chemical → electrical</td><td>Electrical → chemical</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reaction</td><td>Spontaneous</td><td>Non-spontaneous (driven)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Anode</td><td>Oxidation, negative</td><td>Oxidation, positive</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cathode</td><td>Reduction, positive</td><td>Reduction, negative</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Example</td><td>Batteries, fuel cells</td><td>Electroplating, splitting water</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Batteries</strong>, from alkaline cells to the lithium-ion cell in your phone, are galvanic cells; recharging runs them as electrolytic cells.</li>
    <li><strong>Electroplating</strong> coats objects in chromium, nickel, silver or gold.</li>
    <li><strong>Copper refining</strong> dissolves impure copper anodes and deposits pure copper on the cathode.</li>
    <li><strong>Aluminium</strong> is extracted from its molten oxide by electrolysis.</li>
    <li><strong>Hydrogen</strong> for fuel can be made by electrolysis of water using renewable electricity.</li>
    <li><strong>Corrosion protection:</strong> blocks of magnesium or zinc are bolted to ships and pipelines, so that they corrode instead of the steel.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“The anode is always positive.”</strong> It is negative in a galvanic cell and positive in an electrolytic cell; it is always where oxidation happens.</li>
    <li><strong>“Electrons flow through the salt bridge or the solution.”</strong> Ions carry the current there; electrons only travel through the wires and electrodes.</li>
    <li><strong>“A battery stores electricity.”</strong> It stores chemicals that react to produce electricity.</li>
    <li><strong>“Doubling a half-equation doubles E°.”</strong> Electrode potentials do not depend on the amounts written.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    Standard electrode potentials and the Faraday constant are real values, and the Nernst equation is applied with
    concentrations in place of activities, which is only approximate at high concentrations. The internal resistance
    (4 Ω), the bulb (6 Ω), the 100 mL half-cells, the 20 g electrodes and the electrolysis onset voltages are illustrative.
    Time is speeded up (1 s = 1 hour for the galvanic cell, 1 minute for electrolysis), and the drawn electrode sizes
    exaggerate mass changes so they can be seen. Real magnesium also reacts slowly with water, chlorine is partly dissolved
    in the solution, and real cells slow down because of overpotentials and the time ions take to reach the electrodes.
    The ions and electrons are a handful of symbolic dots, not to scale.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Electrochemical cells separate oxidation (at the anode) from reduction (at the cathode).</li>
    <li>In a galvanic cell a spontaneous reaction drives electrons from anode to cathode through the external wire.</li>
    <li>E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode; the voltage falls as the concentrations change.</li>
    <li>Ions, not electrons, carry the charge through solutions and the salt bridge.</li>
    <li>Electrolysis uses electricity to drive non-spontaneous reactions, and the amount of product is proportional to the charge passed.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
